1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,413
Every motorbike in Bagmati carries a number plate.

2
00:00:03,413 --> 00:00:06,746
Thirty players in your class want a five member committee.

3
00:00:06,746 --> 00:00:09,455
Eleven cricketers need a batting order.

4
00:00:09,455 --> 00:00:12,111
How many possibilities are there in each case?

5
00:00:12,111 --> 00:00:13,388
Far too many to list.

6
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:15,984
This whole chapter turns on one question.

7
00:00:15,984 --> 00:00:17,247
Does the order matter?

8
00:00:17,247 --> 00:00:21,323
If it does, you are counting arrangements: permutations.

9
00:00:21,323 --> 00:00:25,164
If it does not, you are counting selections: combinations.

10
00:00:25,164 --> 00:00:30,281
Get that decision right, and every exam question becomes a short calculation.

11
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:41,779
Welcome to Class 12 Mathematics, Unit 1: Permutation and Combination.

12
00:00:41,779 --> 00:00:44,490
Counting, without ever writing out the list.

13
00:00:46,867 --> 00:00:47,689
The plan.

14
00:00:47,689 --> 00:00:51,283
The two basic counting principles and factorials.

15
00:00:51,283 --> 00:00:53,861
Permutations of different objects.

16
00:00:53,861 --> 00:00:55,397
Objects that repeat.

17
00:00:55,397 --> 00:00:58,744
Arrangements round a circle, and with repetition.

18
00:00:58,744 --> 00:01:00,098
Combinations.

19
00:01:00,098 --> 00:01:04,099
And finally the properties of combinations, including Pascal's rule.

20
00:01:08,433 --> 00:01:10,752
Two rules do almost everything.

21
00:01:10,752 --> 00:01:15,987
From Kathmandu to Pokhara you can take one of three buses, or one of two flights.

22
00:01:15,987 --> 00:01:19,463
These are alternatives: you do one or the other.

23
00:01:19,463 --> 00:01:22,596
So the choices add: three plus two, five.

24
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:26,421
Then from Pokhara to Jomsom there are four jeeps.

25
00:01:26,421 --> 00:01:29,000
You do the first leg, and then the second.

26
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:35,757
For each of the five first legs there are four second legs, so the choices multiply: twenty journeys.

27
00:01:35,757 --> 00:01:40,118
The connecting word tells you the rule: OR adds, AND multiplies.

28
00:01:41,333 --> 00:01:43,756
A shorthand we will need constantly.

29
00:01:43,756 --> 00:01:49,303
n factorial is the product of all whole numbers from n down to one.

30
00:01:49,303 --> 00:01:54,394
Five factorial is one hundred and twenty, six factorial seven hundred and twenty.

31
00:01:54,394 --> 00:01:59,819
And notice: n factorial equals n times n minus one factorial.

32
00:02:00,033 --> 00:02:02,352
Put n equal to one in that rule.

33
00:02:02,352 --> 00:02:08,980
One factorial is one times zero factorial, so zero factorial must equal one.

34
00:02:08,980 --> 00:02:11,864
It is not an accident; the formula forces it.

35
00:02:14,067 --> 00:02:20,709
Three boxes: hundreds, tens and units, to be filled from the digits one to six.

36
00:02:20,709 --> 00:02:27,705
If digits may repeat, every box has six choices: six times six times six, two hundred and sixteen.

37
00:02:27,933 --> 00:02:33,664
If they may not repeat, each digit used is gone: six, then five, then four.

38
00:02:33,664 --> 00:02:34,523
One hundred and twenty.

39
00:02:34,733 --> 00:02:39,435
And if the number must also be even, fill the restricted box first.

40
00:02:39,435 --> 00:02:44,852
The units digit must be two, four or six: three choices.

41
00:02:44,852 --> 00:02:48,862
That leaves five for the hundreds and four for the tens.

42
00:02:48,862 --> 00:02:49,425
Sixty.

43
00:02:51,233 --> 00:02:52,602
Exam tip.

44
00:02:52,602 --> 00:03:00,961
Whenever a question has a restriction, must be even, must start with a vowel, a certain person at the head, fill that place first.

45
00:03:00,961 --> 00:03:04,103
Otherwise you will not know how many choices are left for it.

46
00:03:08,467 --> 00:03:09,979
Now the general case.

47
00:03:09,979 --> 00:03:15,031
Arrange r objects, chosen from n different objects, in a row.

48
00:03:15,031 --> 00:03:17,596
The first box can take any of the n.

49
00:03:17,596 --> 00:03:21,815
The second, n minus one, because one is used.

50
00:03:21,815 --> 00:03:23,937
The third, n minus two.

51
00:03:23,937 --> 00:03:27,555
And the r th box takes n minus r plus one.

52
00:03:27,767 --> 00:03:30,425
Multiply them, by the AND rule.

53
00:03:30,425 --> 00:03:41,239
That product is written n P r. Multiply top and bottom by n minus r factorial, and it closes up neatly: n factorial over n minus r factorial.

54
00:03:42,467 --> 00:03:49,565
Put r equal to n: n different objects can be arranged among themselves in n factorial ways.

55
00:03:49,565 --> 00:03:53,081
And n P zero is one, n P one is n.

56
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:55,591
A worked example.

57
00:03:55,591 --> 00:03:58,369
Write both sides as products, not factorials.

58
00:03:58,600 --> 00:04:03,348
n P four is four falling factors; n P two is two.

59
00:04:03,567 --> 00:04:08,888
Cancel n times n minus one, which is not zero, and expand.

60
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:15,208
So n is seven or minus two, and a count cannot be negative: n is seven.

61
00:04:15,208 --> 00:04:23,940
Check: seven P four is eight forty, and twenty times seven P two is twenty times forty two, also eight forty.

62
00:04:24,167 --> 00:04:31,508
And ten P r equal to seven twenty: ten times nine times eight already gives seven twenty, so r is three.

63
00:04:33,533 --> 00:04:35,813
Arrange the letters of TRIANGLE.

64
00:04:35,813 --> 00:04:40,358
Eight different letters: five consonants and three vowels.

65
00:04:40,358 --> 00:04:45,006
With no restriction, eight factorial: forty thousand three hundred and twenty.

66
00:04:47,233 --> 00:04:49,240
Now all the vowels together.

67
00:04:49,240 --> 00:04:52,287
Tie the three vowels into one block.

68
00:04:52,287 --> 00:04:57,664
The block and the five consonants make six units: six factorial ways.

69
00:04:57,664 --> 00:05:02,664
Inside the block, the vowels can be shuffled in three factorial ways.

70
00:05:02,664 --> 00:05:04,808
Four thousand three hundred and twenty.

71
00:05:07,033 --> 00:05:09,105
And no two vowels together.

72
00:05:09,105 --> 00:05:10,472
Use the gap method.

73
00:05:10,472 --> 00:05:15,147
Arrange the five consonants first: five factorial ways.

74
00:05:15,147 --> 00:05:19,522
They leave six gaps: before, between and after them.

75
00:05:19,522 --> 00:05:25,264
Drop the three vowels into three different gaps: six P three ways.

76
00:05:25,264 --> 00:05:29,963
One hundred and twenty times one hundred and twenty: fourteen thousand four hundred.

77
00:05:31,167 --> 00:05:32,601
Careful with the wording.

78
00:05:32,601 --> 00:05:38,278
Vowels not all together is the total minus the block case: thirty six thousand.

79
00:05:38,278 --> 00:05:43,682
No two vowels together needs the gap method: fourteen thousand four hundred.

80
00:05:43,682 --> 00:05:44,901
They are different questions.

81
00:05:49,700 --> 00:05:51,394
Arrange A, A, B.

82
00:05:51,394 --> 00:05:57,709
If we label the two A's to make them different, there are three factorial, six arrangements.

83
00:05:57,709 --> 00:06:01,329
But swapping the labels changes nothing that you can see.

84
00:06:01,329 --> 00:06:07,526
So the six fall into pairs, and there are really only three: six over two factorial.

85
00:06:07,733 --> 00:06:22,097
In general: if n objects contain p alike of one kind, q of another and s of a third, each real arrangement hides p factorial times q factorial times s factorial labelled ones.

86
00:06:22,097 --> 00:06:27,665
So the count is n factorial over p factorial q factorial s factorial.

87
00:06:28,867 --> 00:06:29,580
KATHMANDU.

88
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:33,734
Nine letters, and only A repeats, twice.

89
00:06:33,734 --> 00:06:39,208
Nine factorial over two factorial: one hundred and eighty one thousand four hundred and forty.

90
00:06:39,433 --> 00:06:47,853
Vowels together: A, A and U make one block, plus six different consonants: seven units, seven factorial.

91
00:06:48,067 --> 00:06:54,579
Inside the block, A A U can be ordered in three factorial over two factorial, three ways.

92
00:06:54,579 --> 00:06:56,802
Fifteen thousand one hundred and twenty.

93
00:06:58,833 --> 00:07:00,397
The classic: MISSISSIPPI.

94
00:07:00,600 --> 00:07:06,122
Eleven letters: one M, four I's, four S's, two P's.

95
00:07:06,122 --> 00:07:13,567
Eleven factorial over four factorial four factorial two factorial: thirty four thousand six hundred and fifty.

96
00:07:13,767 --> 00:07:21,629
For no two I's together, arrange the other seven letters first: one hundred and five ways, leaving eight gaps.

97
00:07:21,833 --> 00:07:28,019
The four I's are identical, so we only choose which four gaps: eight C four, seventy.

98
00:07:28,019 --> 00:07:30,279
Seven thousand three hundred and fifty.

